      "writtenOn": "2019-02-26T06:16:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I appreciate the intention, but I think you can do this in a vastly simpler, faster and easier to understand way.\n\nEXPIRYDATE is in the scale of System.currentTimeMillis(), and is the expiry date. In other words, the list of expired records is all where EXPIRYDATE \u003c System.currentTimeMillis. Then, the list of keys to delete is :\n\ndb.query(NetworkAttributesContract.TABLENAME, [L2KEY], COLNAME_EXPIRYDATE + \" \u003c ?\", [System.currentTimeMillis()])\n\n\nIf you feel fancy, you can even install a trigger at the start :\n\n  CREATE TRIGGER delete_cascade_to_private DELETE ON `NetworkAttributesContract.TABLENAME` BEGIN DELETE FROM `PrivateDataContract.TABLENAME` WHERE OLD.`NetworkAttributesContract.COLNAME_L2KEY` \u003d `PrivateDataContract.COLNAME_L2KEY`; END;\n\nOnce this is done, any DELETE on the NetworkAttributes table will delete the row with the same key in PrivateData, if any. Therefore you could afford to simply say :\n\n  DELETE FROM `NetworkAttributesContract.TABLENAME` WHERE COLNAME_EXPIRYDATE \u003c ?\n  (bind ? to System.currentTimeMillis)\n\n...or in Java :\n\n  db.delete(NetworkAttributesContract.TABLENAME, NetworkAttributesContract.COLNAME_L2KEY + \"\u003c ?\", new String[] { Long.toString(System.currentTimeMillis()) } );\n\n\n\n\nNote that as of now, the only case where we delete stuff is when it\u0027s expired. Therefore it\u0027s safe to install the trigger at table creation time (you\u0027ll need to bump up the schema version so that existing devices recreate their tables). But if in the future there is some case where we may want to delete a row from NetworkAttributes without deleting it from PrivateData, then this won\u0027t work, and you\u0027d have to enclose setting the trigger in a transaction : begin transaction, install the trigger, perform the delete, remove the trigger, end transaction.\n\n\nNote there may be other ways to delete from multiple tables in one statement. Some SQL engines support \"DELETE FROM table1, table2 WHERE...\" or \"DELETE FROM table1 LEFT JOIN table2 WHERE...\", but I just tried and it seems SQLite does not support this. There might be other ways that I\u0027m not aware of (I\u0027m not an SQL expert at all.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 503,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 503,
        "endChar": 36
      },
