      "lineNbr": 471,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-06T14:39:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. I think it\u0027s a matter of outlook : you\u0027re trying to drop as little as possible and get as close to the limit as possible. That\u0027s a nice consideration.\n\nHowever I think that if the device is in a state where the DB size exceeds the limit is already a pathological case where the amount of data is growing too fast. It\u0027s very likely such a device will fill up the space again pretty soon – in fact, if you managed to remove exactly as many records as necessary to get under the cap, the next insertion would likely push you over the cap again.\n\nI think it\u0027s better to tip the bias the other way. It doesn\u0027t really matter if we delete a bit too much I think. For example if the DB is 12MB when maintenance starts, it\u0027s fine if it\u0027s 8MB or 9MB when it\u0027s done.\n\nAt least I would like to avoid the pattern where you always delete only half what\u0027s needed because that would make you loop for a long time. I think the 0.5 factor is best left out.",
      "parentUuid": "087de9f1_97744203",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 471,
        "startChar": 32,
        "endLine": 471,
        "endChar": 95
      },
